    "narrative": "The data for 2026-09-17 highlights **Mickey Moniak** as our premier option, commanding an elite 75.98 efficiency score. His placement at the top of the board is driven by an exceptional confluence of a favorable park factor (+14%) and a strong platoon advantage (+14%) against the opposing starter. While the opposing pitcher\u2019s ability to hold runners limits his pure speed upside by 2%, **Mickey Moniak** remains the primary engine for our offensive output today. **Isaac Paredes** follows closely with a 65.76 score; his profile is bolstered by a soft matchup against an arm with a 4.59 xERA, allowing his plate discipline and power potential to shine despite a slight park penalty.\n\nThe remainder of our active lineup occupies the \"Marginal\" to \"Strong\" transition zone. **Brice Turang** (55.40) maintains his status as a key catalyst, as his leadoff role (+10%) offsets the friction created by an elite opposing catcher who suppresses stolen base success. We are keeping **Moises Ballesteros** (54.38) and **Trevor Story** (51.05) in the mix as they clear the threshold for participation; however, **Trevor Story** remains a point of concern as he struggles against neutral left-handed pitching. Regarding our bench, the decision was simplified by a league-wide off-day for our heavy hitters like **Corbin Carroll**, **Jose Caballero**, **Josh Naylor**, and **James Wood**. With those speed threats unavailable, we are forced to rely on **Mickey Moniak** to carry the weight of our stolen base requirements. We continue to monitor the status of **Seiya Suzuki**, **Austin Riley**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Max Muncy**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Griffin Conine**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Cole Young** for potential rotation updates as the season approaches its final stretch.",
